    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d111">

                <pb facs="d111-01" n="1" xml:id="d111-01"/>

                <head>111. Memorandum From the Executive Secretary of State (Springsteen) to the
                    President’s Deputy Assistant for National Security Affairs (Scowcroft)<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d111-f1"/>
                    <anchor n="2" xml:id="d111-f2"/>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1974-09-04"
                        >September 4, 1974</date>
                </dateline>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>Subject:</label>
                    <item>Issues Paper on the Horn of Africa</item>
                </list>
                <p>Attached is an issues paper for the President’s briefing by the Secretary, which
                    describes the situation in the Horn of Africa, with particular attention to the
                    internal situation in Ethiopia, the Somalia-Ethiopia antagonism, and the U.S.
                    military assistance program to Ethiopia.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>George S. Springsteen</signed>
                </closer>


                <xi:include href="d111-a1.xml" xpointer="d111-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d111-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d111-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Ford Library, National Security Adviser, NSC Institutional Files, Box 4,
                    IF/NS File for the President, Horn of Africa Issues Paper. Confidential</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d111-f2" n="2" xml:id="d111-f2-content">
                <p>Springsteen transmitted an analysis of the internal situation in Ethiopia,
                    Somalia-Ethiopia antagonism, and the U.S. military assistance program to
                    Ethiopia in preparation for the President’s briefing by Secretary of State
                    Kissinger.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
